{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786969131,"nonce":"U4MPrK770UkXgxBc0yAYjvXCs18=","title":"Kumo as a group boundary failed two placements and is parked, with the reasons recorded so it is not re-derived","message":"Nothing is broken and nothing needs to ship. This records a design question that was explored, failed twice for the same reason, and was deliberately parked — so the next person to reach for it starts from what was learned rather than from the idea.\n\n**The demonstration.** Grouped alert lists separate one group from the next with the same 1 px `--oto-border` hairline that separates one row from the next — `web/src/features/alerts/GroupedAlerts.tsx`, `web/src/routes/groups.tsx`. A group boundary and a row boundary are different weights of fact drawn identically, so the eye has to read the header text to know a group ended.\n\nKumo — suyari-gumo, the trailing mist band that separates one scene from the next in an emaki scroll — is the motif whose traditional job *is* this job, which is why it was the strongest mapping in the study and why it kept not working.\n\n**Why it matters.** Two placements were built and both were rejected, for reasons that will recur:\n\n*As a free-standing band in the gap between groups.* A band sitting between the last row of one group and the header of the next has nothing to belong to, and reads as debris floating in the gap rather than as a boundary. It also cannot cap the first group — there is no gap above the first header — so group one silently reads as a different kind of header from every other group.\n\n*As the group header's top border.* This fixes both: the ornament belongs to the header, travels with the group when the list reorders or filters, and the first group gets one too. It was still too loud at `--oto-border-strong` and 11 px, competing with the group name it is supposed to introduce, and reducing it far enough to stop competing left something too faint to be worth the asset.\n\nOne rule did survive both attempts and is worth keeping whatever happens here: **a drawn boundary must own its boundary.** Whichever way the band is placed, the row above it gives up its `border-bottom`, or the result is a hairline with a cloud stuck to it — two boundaries where there is one.\n\n**Where.** `web/src/features/alerts/GroupedAlerts.tsx` and `web/src/routes/groups.tsx` (the group headers and the boundary in question); `web/src/design/tokens.css:28-29` (`--oto-border` and `--oto-border-strong`, the two weights tried); `docs/design/SPEC.md:4922-4935` (§M.3 — nothing here is forbidden, the band is Tier A and carries no fact, so this is a taste decision rather than a rules one).\n\n**Scope.** deferred — no work is queued. Reopening it means picking a weight and a placement, not writing code.\n\n**Done when.** Either a placement and a weight are chosen and this becomes an implementation ticket, or the boundary stays a hairline and this closes as declined. It should not be reopened as \"try kumo as a divider\" — that was the first attempt, and the reason it failed is above.","files":null}]}